
@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_DataTable}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_Usage}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Basic_Usage}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Data_Table @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Usage_Basic</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Data_Table @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Usage_Basic</h1>

<p>
    The following example is a basic setup, similar to the one I'd use with results retrieved from the server, however, I wouldn't be using 100K rows, 
    as in the example - quite the opposite. I'd retrieve as few rows as possible, so there's less for the user to have to go through.
</p>
<p>
    For reasons beyond me, it seems to be in vogue to use 100K rows for demos, which, in my experience and opinion, is a little silly given that it's probably only around
    1% or 2% of applications that actually need 100K rows on screen for the user. But to keep you all happy.
</p>
<p>
    As this site is just hosted on GitHub Pages, I simply generated 100K rows rather than calling an API. As a side note, from years of experience, the maximum number of rows
    I'd ever want to retrieve from an API and pull down to a SPA is around 3K to 7K, depending on the number of columns - but generally those numbers are fast.
</p>

<DataTable TData="Contact" DataSource="@_dataSource" Title="Contact Results" PagerBinding="@_pagerBinding" FilterRule="_filterRule">
    <Filter>
        <DebounceFilter HintText="Filters across first name, surname and country" RegexPattern="^[A-Za-z ]*$" OnDebounceFilterResult="HandleOnDebounce"
                        ValidationMessage="Invalid filter, filtering paused, letters and spaces only"  />
    </Filter>
    <TableColumns>
        <DataColumn DataProperty="c => c.ContactID"   DisplayName="ID"            IsSortable="true" />
        <DataColumn DataProperty="c => c.GivenName"   DisplayName="First Name"    IsSortable="true" />
        <DataColumn DataProperty="c => c.FamilyName"  DisplayName="Surname"       IsSortable="true" />
        <DataColumn DataProperty="c => c.Country"     DisplayName="Country"       IsSortable="true" />
        <DataColumn DataProperty="c => c.DateOfBirth" DisplayName="Date of Birth" ColumnAlignment="ColumnAlignment.Centre" IsSortable="true"  />
        <DataColumn DataProperty="c => c.Rate" DisplayName="Hourly Rate" IsSortable="true" CellFormat="C" ColumnAlignment="ColumnAlignment.End" />
    </TableColumns>
    <BottomPager>
        <Pager @bind-CurrentPage="@_pagerBinding.CurrentPage" AriaLabel="Contacts pager" TotalItemCount="@_pagerBinding.TotalItemCount" CurrentItemCount="@_pagerBinding.CurrentItemCount"
        ItemsPerPage="@_pagerBinding.ItemsPerPage" PagerSelectorType="PagerSelectorType.Button" ShowFirstLast="true" PageAlignment="PageAlignment.End" AddApplicationRole="true" />
    </BottomPager>
</DataTable>
<p>
    The Data Table doesn't come with a pager or filtering mechanism — you simply add your own, or drop in the BlazorRamp Debounce Filter and Pager, as I've done here. 
    They all work together and make all the appropriate screen reader announcements for whatever's being used. The demo filter just uses Where the First Name contains OR Surname
    contains OR Country contains the filter value (see code snippet below).
</p>
<p>
    I will at some stage be creating an advanced filter component that you will be able to drop into the data table filter slot, and/or for you to use your own adhoc tables.
</p>
<p>
    I haven't done any performance testing or tuning; the code just uses simple reflection without source generators, so I may revisit this at some point. The sort on WASM 
    takes approx. 1 second for 100K rows with extra delay due to the busy indicator and approx 30 milliseconds on Server. Filtering depends on the number of columns and And-ing or Or-ing. 
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> the Data Table has a <span class="code-span">TableHeight</span> parameter; when using paging this value constrains the maximum height that the entire 
    table element can grow to, with a vertical scrollbar appearing if there is not enough height for the chosen items per page value. When using Virtualized rows, this is a
    fixed height, necessary for the Virtualized component. The default value is 500px (this is a string value so you could change it to 50vh etc).
</p>
<p>
    The code from the example above.
</p>
<CodeBox CodeBlock="@DataTableSnippets.Basic_Usage_Example" AriaLabel="Basic usage code example." />


